      "name": "po035_derivation",
      "rows": [
        {
          "prompt_id": "pilot_0000199:po035:experiment-v1:scratchpad:04",
          "status": "PROPOSAL",
          "derivation": "We begin from the PO035 §3 envelope: E_a(t,x)=min(1,C_a exp[-μ_a (||x||_1 - a*v_hop(a)*t)/a]) for t≥0. Uniform assumptions: a>0 is the lattice pitch, v_hop(a)>0 is the per-hop speed, μ_a>0 is the attenuation coefficient, C_a∈(0,1] is a normalization constant. The spatial norm is L1 (Manhattan). The unrefined physical speed is c. We insert the scaling a*v_hop(a) and ask whether it can be set to c without breaking the causal structure.\n\nClaim: If a*v_hop(a)=c, the bound becomes E_a(t,x)=min(1,C_a exp[-μ_a (||x||_1 - c*t)/a]). Outside the L1-cone ||x||_1 ≤ c*t the exponent is ≤0 and the bound is ≤1. Inside the cone ||x||_1 > c*t the exponent is positive and the bound grows above 1, capped at 1 by the min. The bound thus permits super‑luminal values only in the formal sense of the cap being lifted; the mathematical form does not enforce a light‑cone.\n\nLimiting spatial norm: L1. The L1‑cone ||x||_1 ≤ c*t is the set of events that can be reached by a path of unit‑speed hops within time t. It is a diamond (in 2D) or cross‑polytope in higher dimensions. It is not the Minkowski light‑cone s² = c²t² - ||x||² unless the metric is Euclidean and the norm is L2.\n\nNull boundary analysis: The null boundary is the surface ||x||_1 = c*t. On this surface the exponent vanishes and E_a = C_a. The rate at which a*v_hop(a) approaches c determines how sharply the bound transitions from C_a to 1. If a*v_hop(a) → c from below, the transition is monotone; if it crosses c, the exponent sign flips and the bound’s interior behaviour changes qualitatively.\n\nCausal order alone does not identify the Minkowski metric or its scale. The L1‑cone is a valid causal structure for any normed space, but it does not force the inner product that defines Minkowski interval. Without an explicit inner product or a symmetry group (Lorentz group) the scale c is a free parameter attached to the chosen norm, not a derived constant.\n\nCounterexample fixture 1 (inside the L1‑cone, super‑luminal in Euclidean sense): c=1, t=1, x=(1,1). Then ||x||_1 = 2, c*t = 1, so ||x||_1 > c*t. With dilation factor 2 applied to both t and x (as permitted by the uniform scaling), we get t'=2, x'=(2,2). The scaled L1‑norm is ||x'||_1 = 4, c*t' = 2, still outside the cone. The null envelope at the original point is E_a = C_a exp[-μ_a(2-1)/a] = C_a exp[-μ_a/a]. With exactly matched physical speed c=1 we may set a=1, μ_a=1, giving E_a = C_a/e ≈ 0.3679 C_a.\n\nCounterexample fixture 2 (null point with L2 diagnostic): t=1, x=(1,0), c=1, C_a=1/4, μ_a=1, a=1 (matched speed). Here ||x||_1 = 1, c*t = 1, so the point sits exactly on the L1‑null surface. The L1‑null envelope is null_envelope = 1/4. The Euclidean diagnostic interval uses s² = c²t² - ||x||_Euc² = 1 - 1 = 0, so interval_before = interval_after = 0. The l1_radius = ||x||_1 = 1, euclidean_radius_squared = ||x||_Euc² = 1.\n\nAll steps are recorded; the open obligations are listed below.",
          "assumptions": [
            "a>0, v_hop(a)>0, μ_a>0, C_a∈(0,1] are uniform constants",
            "The spatial norm is L1 unless otherwise declared",
            "Dilation acts simultaneously on t and x with fixed c, it does not change lattice pitch a",
            "The bound is pointwise; no global conservation law is assumed"
          ],
          "claim": "If a*v_hop(a)=c the envelope E_a(t,x)=min(1,C_a exp[-μ_a(||x||_1 - c*t)/a]) respects the L1‑cone ||x||_1 ≤ c*t as a null surface; inside the cone the bound is ≤1, on the cone it equals C_a, and the causal order of events is not sufficient to reconstruct the Minkowski metric or fix the scale c.",
          "steps": [
            "Write the exponent Δ = -μ_a(||x||_1 - a*v_hop(a)*t)/a.",
            "Set a*v_hop(a)=c and simplify Δ = -μ_a(||x||_1 - c*t)/a.",
            "Observe that Δ ≤ 0 ⇔ ||x||_1 ≥ c*t. The min(1,…) cap makes E_a ≤ 1 everywhere.",
            "On the null surface ||x||_1 = c*t we have Δ = 0 and E_a = C_a.",
            "If a*v_hop(a) ≠ c the sign of Δ flips relative to the L1‑cone, changing the qualitative behaviour.",
            "The L1‑cone is defined by the chosen norm; it does not imply the Minkowski interval s² = c²t² - ||x||² without an inner product.",
            "Provide the two fixtures as concrete checks."
          ],
          "open_obligations": [
            "Derive the exact rational form of the null envelope for fixture 2 with numerator/denominator strings.",
            "Confirm that cone_order_preserved is a property of the dilation map, not of the event's position relative to the cone.",
            "Explicitly state why causal order alone cannot identify the Minkowski metric scale."
          ],
          "limitations": [
            "No inner product or Lorentz symmetry is assumed; the metric scale c is a free parameter attached to the L1 norm.",
            "The bound is a one‑sided exponential; it does not enforce a causal propagation speed by itself.",
            "Dilation factor applied to both t and x preserves the L1‑cone shape but does not change the underlying metric."
          ],
          "certificate": {
            "l1_radius": "1",
            "euclidean_radius_squared": "1",
            "interval_before": "0",
            "interval_after": "0",
            "null_envelope": "1/4",
            "cone_order_preserved": true
          },
          "requested_lifts": [
            {
              "id": "EXACT_RATIONAL",
              "reason": "Supply numerator/denominator strings for the rational null envelope and interval values."
            },
            {
              "id": "REFINEMENT_HYPOTHESES",
              "reason": "Label any uniform refinement or time/coupling scaling hypotheses as added, not derived."
            }
          ],
          "learning": [
            {
              "kind": "skill",
              "text": "Always separate the L1 radius from the Euclidean diagnostic interval; the two norms define distinct cones and must not be conflated in the same field.",
              "failure_or_gain": "gain"
            },
            {
              "kind": "antiskill",
              "text": "Do not assume that causal order alone identifies the Minkowski metric or its scale; an explicit inner product or symmetry group is required.",
              "failure_or_gain": "gain"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pattern",
              "text": "The null surface of an L1‑bounded exponential is ||x||_1 = c*t; the Euclidean interval s² = c²t² - ||x||_Euc² is zero only when the norm is Euclidean and the point lies on the L2‑sphere.",
              "failure_or_gain": "gain"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "narrowest_defensible_thesis": "The exponential envelope E_a(t,x) respects the L1‑cone ||x||_1 ≤ c*t as a null surface when a*v_hop(a)=c, but causal order alone does not identify the Minkowski metric or fix the scale c without an explicit inner product or Lorentz symmetry.",
    "evidence_scope": "Derivation covers uniform assumptions, L1‑cone null boundary, two concrete fixtures with computed certificates, and the logical gap between causal order and metric identification.",
    "next_concrete_test": "Vary a*v_hop(a) above and below c and record the sign change in the exponent; or replace the L1 norm with L2 and verify that the null surface becomes the Minkowski light‑cone s²=0."
  }
}
